  "Question": "After the line \\(y=x-1\\) is translated upward by 4 units, what is the y-coordinate of its intersection with the y-axis?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "3",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The equation after translation is \\(y=x-1+4=x+3\\). Setting \\(x=0\\) gives \\(y=3\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Translate the intercept point\n\nA vertical translation leaves each point's \\(x\\)-coordinate unchanged, so the original intersection with the \\(y\\)-axis moves to the new intersection. Setting \\(x=0\\) in the original equation gives \\(y=-1\\). Moving \\((0,-1)\\) upward by 4 units gives \\((0,-1+4)=(0,3)\\), so the required \\(y\\)-coordinate is \\(3\\).",
